The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (cifs_sb->root) {
    dput(cifs_sb->root);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `cifs_kill_sb` function is called even during mount failures, leading to the release of cached dentries (including the root dentry) before confirming that the filesystem mount is complete. If the mount fails, the cached dentries may not be valid, resulting in dereferencing invalid pointers and potential memory corruption.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (cifs_sb->root) {
    node = rb_first(root);
    while (node != NULL) {
        ...
    }
    dput(cifs_sb->root);
    cifs_sb->root = NULL;
}
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that cached dentries are only released if the filesystem mount is confirmed to be complete (i.e., `cifs_sb->root` is populated). By checking for `cifs_sb->root` before releasing the cached dentries, the code prevents premature dereferencing of potentially invalid pointers, thus avoiding memory corruption during mount failures.

